dogma_v1 utf-8
- identifier  = parquet_v1
- description = Apache Parquet columnar storage format
- reference   = https://parquet.apache.org/docs/file-format/
- dogma       = https://github.com/kstenerud/dogma/blob/master/v1/dogma_v1.0.md

# ==============================================================================
# Apache Parquet File Format
# ==============================================================================
#
# Parquet is a columnar storage format: data is organized by columns rather
# than rows, so similar values sit together (good compression) and a query can
# read only the columns it needs.
#
# Scope and how to use this document:
#   - Sections 1-9 describe the wire format, NOT a promise that parquet4j
#     implements every feature. Section 10 maps the supported subset to code;
#     section 11 separates corrected documentation from compatibility gaps.
#   - The diagram shows the common, unencrypted, single-file layout. Metadata
#     offsets, not adjacency or diagram order, locate chunks and side structures.
#   - Dogma rules are a structural scaffold with prose-defined functions, not
#     a complete parser generator input: Thrift fields, schema-dependent lengths,
#     compression, and EOF-relative lookup require the described procedures.
#     `&` means concatenation, `|` choice, `?` optional, `*` zero-or-more,
#     `+` one-or-more, `{n}` exactly n repetitions. `u8(~)` is any one byte.
#   - FileMetaData.version, DATA_PAGE versus DATA_PAGE_V2, and the version of
#     the upstream specification/library are THREE DIFFERENT version numbers.
#     A version=1 footer may describe V2 pages and modern logical annotations.
#
# Reading routes:
#   First implementation: 1-3 (framing), 4 (levels), 5 (metadata), 6-8 (bytes).
#   Nested data: 4a/4b/4c. Byte debugging: 3e, 6c, 6d, 8.
#   Repository coverage: 10. Unresolved mismatches: 11. Validation checklist: 12.
#
# A Parquet file commonly looks like this:
#
#   +--------------------------+
#   | "PAR1"                   |  4-byte magic number (file header)
#   +--------------------------+
#   | Row group 0              |  horizontal slice of the table
#   |   Column chunk A         |  all values of leaf column A in this row group
#   |     [dictionary page]    |  optional, at most one per chunk, always first
#   |     data page 0          |  PageHeader + body
#   |     data page 1          |
#   |   Column chunk B         |
#   |     ...                  |
#   | Row group 1              |
#   |   ...                    |
#   +--------------------------+
#   | [page indexes / blooms]  |  optional side structures, found by offsets
#   +--------------------------+
#   | FileMetaData             |  Thrift Compact Protocol bytes (the "footer
#   |                          |  metadata"): schema, row groups, statistics
#   +--------------------------+
#   | footer length (u32 LE)   |  byte length of the FileMetaData above
#   +--------------------------+
#   | "PAR1"                   |  4-byte magic number (file footer)
#   +--------------------------+
#
# Key vocabulary:
#   - Row group:     a horizontal partition of rows. Unit of parallelism and of
#                    I/O batching; typically 64 MiB - 1 GiB of column data.
#   - Column chunk:  all the data of ONE leaf column within ONE row group,
#                    stored as one contiguous run of pages.
#   - Page:          the unit of compression and encoding within a chunk.
#   - Leaf column:   a primitive (non-group) node of the schema tree. Nested
#                    structures (LIST/MAP/STRUCT) are flattened into leaves;
#                    repetition/definition levels (section 4) rebuild nesting.
#   - Levels:        two integer streams per data page (repetition levels and
#                    definition levels) that describe nesting and nulls.
#
# Reading a file (the standard recipe):
#   1. Check the first 4 bytes are "PAR1".
#   2. Seek to the end: read the last 8 bytes = [u32 LE footer length]["PAR1"].
#   3. footer_meta_start = file_length - 8 - footer_length; read footer_length
#      bytes there and Thrift-decode them as FileMetaData.
#   4. FileMetaData.schema describes the columns (depth-first tree).
#   5. For each RowGroup, for each ColumnChunk, seek to its first page offset
#      and walk PageHeader+body pairs until the chunk's bytes are consumed.
#
# A decoder's pipeline is: locate chunk -> parse PageHeader -> bound/read body
# -> verify optional CRC on stored bytes -> decompress the appropriate region
# -> decode levels -> count present values -> decode physical values -> apply
# logical annotations -> rebuild nested rows. Null slots never consume values.
# Encoding and compression are separate: PLAIN is an encoding, not a codec.
#
# Metadata structs and page headers use Thrift Compact Protocol. Magic bytes,
# the fixed-width footer length, bloom bitsets, compressed bodies, and encrypted
# modules do NOT. There is no Thrift RPC message envelope in a Parquet struct.
# Integer byte order in page bodies/footer framing is little-endian unless
# stated otherwise; Thrift integers use zigzag varints, not fixed-width LE.
#
# Authoritative upstream references (this document summarizes and cross-checks
# them; when in doubt, these win):
#   - https://github.com/apache/parquet-format/blob/master/src/main/thrift/parquet.thrift
#   - https://github.com/apache/parquet-format/blob/master/Encodings.md
#   - https://github.com/apache/parquet-format/blob/master/LogicalTypes.md
#   - https://github.com/apache/parquet-format/blob/master/Compression.md
#   - https://github.com/apache/parquet-format/blob/master/PageIndex.md
#   - https://github.com/apache/parquet-format/blob/master/BloomFilter.md
#   - https://github.com/apache/parquet-format/blob/master/Encryption.md
#
# Reproducible source baseline: apache/parquet-format commit
# bf0993925ccf41b1fb4b1ae241af4a66e8adf1fe (replace "master" in URLs above
# with this commit to freeze the reference). Features newer than the pinned
# 2.9.0 definitions are labeled below.
# parquet4j pins parquet-format-structures 1.13.1 (pom.xml), generated from
# parquet-format 2.9.0, NOT the latest definitions:
#   https://github.com/apache/parquet-format/blob/apache-parquet-format-2.9.0/src/main/thrift/parquet.thrift
#   https://github.com/apache/parquet-java/blob/apache-parquet-1.13.1/pom.xml
# ==============================================================================

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. File structure
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# A file with zero row groups is valid: it still has a schema and a nonempty
# FileMetaData struct with num_rows=0 and an empty row_groups list.
# There is no on-disk RowGroup header or ColumnChunk wrapper around the pages.
# Their boundaries exist in footer metadata. The intervening region can also
# contain auxiliary structures; do not parse it as row_group* until EOF.
parquet_file      = byte_order(lsb, file_structure);

file_structure    = file_header
                  & file_payload
                  & file_footer
                  ;

file_payload: bits =
    """
    Bytes between the four-byte header and metadata_start (section 5).
    Determine metadata_start from the EOF trailer BEFORE parsing this region.
    Use footer offsets/sizes to interpret column pages and optional side data;
    do not assume that every byte here starts a page or a row group.
    """;

# The file begins with a 4-byte magic number "PAR1"
file_header       = magic_number;
magic_number      = "PAR1";

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Row groups and column chunks
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A row group holds N rows (FileMetaData.row_groups[i].num_rows) and contains
# exactly one column chunk per leaf column, in schema leaf order
# (RowGroup.columns must match the FileMetaData.schema leaf order).
#
# A column chunk is a contiguous byte range of the file:
#   [first page offset, first page offset + ColumnMetaData.total_compressed_size)
# Pages are laid out back to back, each as: PageHeader (Thrift) + page body.
# data_page_offset locates the first DATA page; dictionary_page_offset, when
# present, locates its preceding dictionary. Start at the dictionary if present,
# otherwise at data_page_offset. These are absolute byte offsets from file start.
# To walk pages, advance by bytes ACTUALLY consumed by the Thrift PageHeader
# parser plus compressed_page_size. OffsetIndex can also locate data pages.
# The total sizes include headers and dictionary pages, but NOT bloom filters,
# column/offset indexes, footer metadata, or the file's magic/trailer bytes.
# Row groups/columns are logical containers: seek through metadata even when
# the common physical layout happens to follow schema order. Sibling columns
# need not have matching page sizes, counts, or row boundaries.
# The row_group/column_chunk rules below illustrate ordinary page runs; they
# are not used to discover boundaries in the start rule.
#
# If a dictionary page exists it is always the FIRST page of the chunk
# (dictionary_page_offset < data_page_offset), and there is at most one.
row_group         = column_chunk+;

column_chunk      = dictionary_page?
                  & data_page+
                  ;

# Each data page is V1 or V2, declared by its PageHeader.type. A chunk may mix
# versions in theory; all pages of a chunk share ColumnMetaData.codec (V2 pages
# may individually opt out of compression via is_compressed, see section 7).
data_page         = data_page_v1
                  | data_page_v2
                  ;

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. Pages
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Pages are the unit of compression and encoding. Each page is a Thrift-encoded
# PageHeader followed by the page body (compressed_page_size bytes).
#
# PageHeader.uncompressed_page_size is the size of the body AFTER decompression
# (for V2: level bytes + decompressed values bytes); compressed_page_size is
# the size of the body AS STORED. Neither includes the page header itself.
#
# Page types (PageType enum):
#   DATA_PAGE       (0): V1 data page (RLE levels have 4-byte lengths;
#                        legacy BIT_PACKED differs; whole body compressed)
#   INDEX_PAGE      (1): reserved for the (never standardized) index page
#   DICTIONARY_PAGE (2): dictionary values for dictionary encoding
#   DATA_PAGE_V2    (3): V2 data page (levels stored uncompressed and unframed,
#                        only the values region may be compressed)

dictionary_page   = page_header_dict
                  & compressed_dict_data
                  ;

data_page_v1      = page_header_data_v1
                  & compressed_data_page_v1
                  ;

data_page_v2      = page_header_data_v2
                  & page_v2_data
                  ;

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3a. Page headers (Thrift Compact Protocol encoded)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PageHeader (parquet.thrift field ids):
#   1: required PageType type                       which *_header field is set
#   2: required i32   uncompressed_page_size        body size after decompression
#   3: required i32   compressed_page_size          body size as stored
#   4: optional i32   crc                           CRC32 of the stored body
#   5: optional DataPageHeader       data_page_header
#   6: optional IndexPageHeader      index_page_header
#   7: optional DictionaryPageHeader dictionary_page_header
#   8: optional DataPageHeaderV2     data_page_header_v2
#
# Exactly one of fields 5-8 is set, matching `type`.
#
# crc semantics (easy to get wrong):
#   - Algorithm: standard CRC-32 (polynomial 0x04C11DB7, the same as GZIP's;
#     e.g. java.util.zip.CRC32 in Java).
#   - Computed over the page body bytes EXACTLY as stored on disk (i.e. after
#     compression/encryption), NOT over the decompressed bytes, and NOT
#     including the page header.
#   - Optional. A reader policy may choose whether to verify a present CRC;
#     when verified, a mismatch is corruption, not permission to change bytes.
#     Compare the 32-bit bit pattern: Thrift i32 may represent it as negative.
#
# DataPageHeader (V1) - field ids:
#   1: required i32 num_values                values INCLUDING nulls (level count)
#   2: required Encoding encoding             value encoding (see section 6)
#   3: required Encoding definition_level_encoding
#   4: required Encoding repetition_level_encoding
#   5: optional Statistics statistics
#   Fields 3/4 must be RLE (3) for modern files (BIT_PACKED (4) is deprecated);
#   they describe the LEVEL sections, not the values.
#
# DataPageHeaderV2 - field ids:
#   1: required i32 num_values                values INCLUDING nulls (level count)
#   2: required i32 num_nulls                 nulls in this page
#   3: required i32 num_rows                  rows in this page; V2 pages MUST
#                                             begin and end at row boundaries
#                                             (a row is never split across pages)
#   4: required Encoding encoding             value encoding
#   5: required i32 definition_levels_byte_length
#   6: required i32 repetition_levels_byte_length
#   7: optional bool is_compressed = true     "true" if the VALUES region is
#                                             compressed (see section 7)
#   8: optional Statistics statistics
#   NOTE THE TRAP: field order lists definition (5) before repetition (6), but
#   the on-disk body stores REPETITION levels FIRST, then definition levels.
#   NOTE: is_compressed omitted = true (Thrift default). Writers storing raw
#   values must explicitly write false.
#
# DictionaryPageHeader - field ids:
#   1: required i32 num_values                entries in the dictionary
#   2: required Encoding encoding             PLAIN (PLAIN_DICTIONARY deprecated)
#   3: optional bool is_sorted
#
# The header serialization has no length prefix: it is a self-delimiting Thrift
# struct ending with a 0x00 stop byte. The body begins immediately after it.

page_header_dict    = thrift_compact_protocol;
page_header_data_v1 = thrift_compact_protocol;
page_header_data_v2 = thrift_compact_protocol;

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3b. DATA_PAGE (V1) body
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# V1 framing rule: the whole body is ONE compressed unit (when a codec is set,
# everything below is compressed together; compressed_page_size is the stored
# size, uncompressed_page_size the decompressed size).
#
# The following V1 layout assumes both level encodings are RLE. V1 also
# permits legacy BIT_PACKED levels: those have NO four-byte length prefix and
# occupy ceil(num_values * bit_width / 8) bytes per present level section.
# Honor the two header encoding fields independently; an unsupported encoding
# must be rejected, not reinterpreted as RLE. V2 has no such selector.
#
# After decompression the RLE-level body contains, in order:
#   1. Repetition levels  - ONLY IF max_repetition_level > 0
#        4-byte little-endian length (bytes of RLE data that follow)
#        RLE/bit-packed-hybrid encoded level stream (section 6c)
#   2. Definition levels  - ONLY IF max_definition_level > 0
#        4-byte little-endian length (bytes of RLE data that follow)
#        RLE/bit-packed-hybrid encoded level stream (section 6c)
#   3. Encoded values     - present-value encoding from the header's `encoding`
#        (number of encoded values = num_values minus the nulls)
#
# Both level sections are present or absent independently, based on the
# column's max levels (computed from the schema path, section 4). An absent
# section means num_values implicit zero levels, not a missing count.
# V1 has no num_nulls/num_rows: derive present count from definition levels;
# derive row starts from rep=0 and carry row assembly across page boundaries.
# A V1 page can start with rep>0 when it continues a row from the previous page.

compressed_data_page_v1 = u8(~)*;

decompressed_data_page_v1 = repetition_levels_v1?
                          & definition_levels_v1?
                          & encoded_values_v1
                          ;

# Repetition levels in V1 (RLE/bit-packed hybrid with 4-byte LE length prefix)
repetition_levels_v1 = u32(var(rep_len, ~))
                     & u8(~){rep_len}
                     ;

# Definition levels in V1 (RLE/bit-packed hybrid with 4-byte LE length prefix)
definition_levels_v1 = u32(var(def_len, ~))
                     & u8(~){def_len}
                     ;

# Raw RLE/bit-packed-hybrid stream (section 6c); byte length given by caller
rle_hybrid         = u8(~)*;

# Encoded values; layout depends on the encoding named in the page header
encoded_values_v1  = u8(~)*;

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3c. DATA_PAGE_V2 body
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# V2 framing rule: level sections are stored UNCOMPRESSED and UNFRAMED (no
# 4-byte length prefixes - their byte lengths come from DataPageHeaderV2), and
# only the values region may be compressed. The stored body layout is:
#
#   +--------------------------------------+
#   | repetition levels (raw hybrid RLE)   |  repetition_levels_byte_length bytes
#   +--------------------------------------+
#   | definition levels (raw hybrid RLE)   |  definition_levels_byte_length bytes
#   +--------------------------------------+
#   | encoded values                       |  remainder of the body;
#   |                                      |  compressed iff is_compressed
#   +--------------------------------------+
#
# Sizes: compressed_page_size   = rep_len + def_len + stored values bytes
#        uncompressed_page_size = rep_len + def_len + decoded values bytes
# (level byte lengths are identical in both sizes because levels are raw)
#
# A level section is empty (length 0) when its max level is 0, supplying
# num_values implicit zero levels. parquet4j also accepts explicit width-0
# hybrid streams as a compatibility extension (not required writer output).
# For repeated columns, a conforming V2 page starts with rep=0 and num_rows
# equals the number of zero repetition levels. For flat columns, num_rows is
# num_values. num_nulls counts ALL slots with def<max_def, including null/empty
# enclosing containers; it is NOT necessarily a count of null top-level rows.
# Subtract the level lengths from BOTH sizes before decompressing the values.
# Empty/all-null pages may have no physical values; do not manufacture one.

page_v2_data       = repetition_levels_v2
                  & definition_levels_v2
                  & encoded_values_v2
                  ;

# Repetition levels in V2 (uncompressed, byte length from page header)
repetition_levels_v2 = u8(~)*;

# Definition levels in V2 (uncompressed, byte length from page header)
definition_levels_v2 = u8(~)*;

# Encoded values in V2 (compressed iff is_compressed is true / omitted)
encoded_values_v2  = u8(~)*;

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3d. DICTIONARY_PAGE body
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# After decompression, the body holds num_values dictionary entries encoded
# back to back with PLAIN (section 6a). Plain encoding here means, e.g., that a
# BYTE_ARRAY dictionary is a run of [u32 LE length][bytes] entries.
#
# Data pages then reference entries by index using RLE_DICTIONARY (8) or the
# deprecated PLAIN_DICTIONARY (2); see section 6b.

compressed_dict_data = u8(~)*;

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3e. Byte-exact example: optional INT32 [10, null, 20]
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Schema: optional int32 id (max_def=1, max_rep=0), PLAIN, UNCOMPRESSED.
# This deliberately minimal V2 page has no statistics/CRC; it is legal even
# though parquet4j normally writes statistics. All bytes below are hexadecimal.
#
# Body (14 bytes):
#   repetition: empty (implicit [0,0,0])
#   definition: 02 01 02 00 02 01   (three one-value repeated runs -> [1,0,1])
#   values:     0A 00 00 00 14 00 00 00   (10 and 20; NO null payload)
# Header (22 bytes, Compact-serialized PageHeader):
#   15 06 15 1C 15 1C 5C
#   15 06 15 02 15 06 15 00 15 0C 15 00 12 00 00
# Decode: type=3, uncompressed_page_size=14, compressed_page_size=14;
# field 8 is the nested V2 struct: num_values=3, num_nulls=1, num_rows=3,
# encoding=0, def_len=6, rep_len=0, is_compressed=false (header byte 12).
# The two final STOP bytes close the V2 struct and then PageHeader.
# Whole page/chunk size is 36 bytes. If this is the only chunk immediately
# after PAR1, data_page_offset=4 and it ends at offset 40; footer starts there
# if there are no side structures. Footer length still counts ONLY metadata.
#
# The equivalent V1 UNCOMPRESSED body using RLE levels is 18 bytes:
#   06 00 00 00 | 02 01 02 00 02 01 | 0A 00 00 00 14 00 00 00
# There is NO repetition prefix at max_rep=0. Its DataPageHeader has count=3,
# encoding=PLAIN and both level encodings=RLE; its page type is DATA_PAGE (0).
# Readers must return [10,null,20] in both cases, not [10,20,null].
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. Repetition and definition levels (Dremel)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Leaf columns of nested schemas carry two integer streams per data page that
# describe, per "value slot", how the value nests and where nulls live. One
# slot = one entry in each level stream = one unit of num_values. A slot whose
# definition level is below the column's maximum carries NO encoded value (the
# value is null at that nesting depth); the values stream contains only
# non-null values, in slot order.
#
# Definition level (max_definition_level = number of OPTIONAL + REPEATED nodes
# in the path from the root, excluding the root itself):
#   "how many fields in the path to this column are actually defined."
#   - equal to max_definition_level: value is present (encoded in values stream)
#   - less: null (or an enclosing container is null/empty) at that depth
#
# Repetition level (max_repetition_level = number of REPEATED nodes in the path):
#   "at which repeated ancestor the value's row repeats."
#   - 0: start of a new top-level row
#   - r > 0: this slot continues the same row, repeating the ancestor at
#     repetition depth r (used to rebuild lists/maps)
#
# Simple flat-column examples:
#   required int32 id: max_def=0, max_rep=0; no stored levels, every slot present.
#   optional int32 id: max_def=1, max_rep=0; [10,null,20] has def=[1,0,1],
#     implicit rep=[0,0,0], num_values=3, physical values=[10,20].
# A REQUIRED leaf can still have null slots when an enclosing OPTIONAL group
# is absent: required does not make all ancestors required.
#
# Legacy repeated primitive (no LIST annotation):
#   optional group Person { repeated binary phoneNumbers (STRING); }
# has max_def=2, max_rep=1. Person=null -> (def=0,rep=0); Person={} ->
# (def=1,rep=0); ["a","b"] -> (2,0),(2,1). A repeated primitive cannot
# represent a null element inside a nonempty list. Use a three-level LIST
# with OPTIONAL element to distinguish null list, empty list, and null element.
#
# 4a. Standard LIST (new writers must use this three-level shape)
#
#   optional group phones (LIST) {       # def +1: nullable list
#     repeated group list {             # def +1, rep +1: list entries
#       optional binary element (STRING); # def +1: nullable element
#     }
#   }
#
# Leaf path = ["phones","list","element"], max_def=3, max_rep=1.
#   Input rows       def stream     rep stream     stored STRING values
#   null             [0]            [0]            []
#   []               [1]            [0]            []
#   [null]           [2]            [0]            []
#   ["a",null,"b"]   [3,2,3]        [0,1,1]        ["a","b"]
# Together: num_rows=4, num_values=6, present=2, num_nulls=4.
# Required list removes its def increment; required element removes its own.
# Do not decode def=1 as [null]: it means there are NO entries in the list.
# Nested lists add repeated depths: for list<list<T>>, rep=1 starts the next
# inner list in the same outer list; rep=2 continues the same inner list.
#
# Historical LIST compatibility (LogicalTypes.md specifies these, not guesses):
#   - A repeated primitive child is itself the required element type.
#   - A repeated group with multiple fields is itself the required element.
#   - A one-field repeated group whose field is repeated is itself the element.
#   - A repeated group named "array" or "<outer-list-name>_tuple" is itself
#     the required element, even if it has a single field.
#   - Otherwise its single non-repeated child is the element, with that child's
#     repetition determining nullability. Honor these legacy forms in readers;
#     emit the standard `list`/`element` names and shape in new writers.
#
# 4b. Standard MAP
#
#   optional group properties (MAP) {   # def +1: nullable map
#     repeated group key_value {       # def +1, rep +1: map entries
#       required binary key (STRING);  # max_def=2, max_rep=1
#       optional int32 value;          # max_def=3, max_rep=1
#     }
#   }
#
#   Row                  key def / rep       value def / rep     values
#   null                 [0] / [0]           [0] / [0]            none
#   {}                   [1] / [0]           [1] / [0]            none
#   {"x":7,"y":null}     [2,2] / [0,1]       [3,2] / [0,1]       keys x,y; 7
# Keys must be required/non-null; null/empty-map placeholders consume no key.
# Value may be required or optional, and nested values are allowed by the
# format. The spec also permits an omitted value field for a set of keys.
# Duplicate keys use the last encountered value per LogicalTypes.md; engines
# need not preserve entry order. Do not invent additional entries from levels.
# Key/value leaf chunks need not split pages together: synchronize by logical
# row/entry events, never by page index or by physical leaf index == row field.
#
# 4c. STRUCT and count invariants
# A struct is an ordinary group with no special STRUCT logical annotation.
# OPTIONAL group absence produces placeholders in EVERY descendant leaf.
# Different leaves may have different slot counts due to different repeated
# paths; within a leaf, sum(data-page num_values) = ColumnMetaData.num_values.
# For an entire chunk, number of rep=0 slots = RowGroup.num_rows. Do not include
# dictionary entries in that count. FileMetaData.num_rows = sum(group.num_rows).
#
# Level value ranges: 0 <= level <= max_level. Stored streams account for
# exactly num_values slots after discarding up to seven final bit-packed pad
# values; omitted width-zero streams are implicit. Width is bit_length(max_level)
# = ceil(log2(max_level + 1)), with width 0 when max_level=0. Levels use hybrid
# RLE (section 6c), except for legacy V1 BIT_PACKED (sections 3b and 6h).

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. File footer (metadata)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The footer is: FileMetaData (Thrift Compact Protocol) + 4-byte little-endian
# footer length (byte size of that FileMetaData) + "PAR1".
#
# To locate the metadata from EOF:
#   footer_length   = u32 LE at [file_length - 8 .. file_length - 4]
#   magic           = "PAR1"      at [file_length - 4 .. file_length]
#   metadata_start  = file_length - 8 - footer_length
# Validate with wide, overflow-checked arithmetic: file_length >= 12, both
# magics match, footer_length > 0, metadata_start >= 4, metadata ends exactly
# at file_length-8. Equivalently footer_length <= file_length-12; this preserves
# the initial four-byte header. Set an allocation limit BEFORE reading metadata.
# The u32 framing permits lengths above signed-i32 range; a reader unable to
# allocate those must explicitly reject them rather than wrap to negative.

file_footer       = file_metadata
                  & footer_length
                  & magic_number
                  ;

file_metadata     = thrift_compact_protocol;

# Footer length is stored as a 4-byte little-endian integer
footer_length     = u32(~);

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5a. FileMetaData (Thrift struct; field ids matter for forward compatibility)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   1: required i32 version               upstream recommends write 1, read 1/2
#                                         (not a page-version selector)
#   2: required list<SchemaElement> schema   depth-first, schema[0] = root
#   3: required i64 num_rows               total rows in the file
#   4: required list<RowGroup> row_groups
#   5: optional list<KeyValue> key_value_metadata   free-form strings
#   6: optional string created_by          writer identification
#   7: optional list<ColumnOrder> column_orders     one per leaf column
#   8: optional EncryptionAlgorithm encryption_algorithm
#   9: optional binary footer_signing_key_metadata
#
# SchemaElement (the schema tree is flattened depth-first; num_children
# rebuilds the nesting):
#   1: optional Type type                 set for LEAVES only
#   2: optional i32 type_length           required for FIXED_LEN_BYTE_ARRAY
#   3: optional FieldRepetitionType repetition_type  REQUIRED/OPTIONAL/REPEATED;
#                                         unset for the root element only
#   4: required string name
#   5: optional i32 num_children          set for GROUPS only (absent for leaves)
#   6: optional ConvertedType converted_type   DEPRECATED logical annotation
#   7: optional i32 scale                 DEPRECATED (DECIMAL)
#   8: optional i32 precision             DEPRECATED (DECIMAL)
#   9: optional i32 field_id
#  10: optional LogicalType logicalType   modern logical annotation
#
# Rules a reader must apply:
#   - schema[0] is the root: has name + num_children, NO type, NO repetition.
#   - leaves are exactly the elements with `type` set; they appear in the same
#     order as RowGroup.columns and determine ColumnMetaData.path_in_schema.
#   - path_in_schema is the full path from the root's CHILD down to the leaf
#     (root name excluded), one string per level.
#   - When logicalType is set, writers must also set converted_type wherever
#     a legacy equivalent exists (plus DECIMAL scale/precision). Prefer the
#     modern annotation; if they conflict, flag inconsistent schema metadata
#     rather than claiming both interpretations. Unknown logical annotations
#     may be exposed as the underlying physical type (with semantics unknown).
#   - Use num_children to consume precisely each group subtree; reject extra or
#     missing schema nodes, incompatible logical/physical types, invalid fixed
#     widths, and paths/types that disagree with the row-group column metadata.
#
# LogicalType is a Thrift UNION: exactly one alternative (possibly unknown
# to the reader). Common
# union field ids and physical carriers (annotation does not change encoding):
#   1 STRING: BYTE_ARRAY, UTF-8 text; 4 ENUM: BYTE_ARRAY, UTF-8 enum name.
#   2 MAP / 3 LIST: groups with the shapes in section 4, not primitive values.
#   5 DECIMAL: INT32/INT64 store an ordinary little-endian signed unscaled
#     integer; BYTE_ARRAY/FIXED_LEN_BYTE_ARRAY store two's-complement BIG-ENDIAN
#     unscaled bytes. Value = unscaled * 10^-scale. Precision > 0 and
#     0 <= scale <= precision; INT32 precision <= 9, INT64 <= 18. Fixed length n
#     permits floor(log10(2^(8*n-1)-1)) digits. Variable bytes should use the
#     minimum signed representation; sign-extend fixed bytes. Numeric ordering,
#     not unsigned byte ordering, is required for DECIMAL bounds.
#   6 DATE: INT32 signed days since 1970-01-01 (proleptic Gregorian calendar).
#   7 TIME: INT32 for MILLIS, INT64 for MICROS/NANOS; units since midnight,
#     with isAdjustedToUTC plus TimeUnit union (1 MILLIS, 2 MICROS, 3 NANOS).
#   8 TIMESTAMP: INT64 signed units since epoch; isAdjustedToUTC=true denotes
#     an instant, false denotes local wall-clock fields with no time zone.
#     Never infer the original time zone or apply host-zone conversion to a
#     local timestamp. NANOS has no legacy ConvertedType counterpart.
#     Newer upstream also permits FIXED_LEN_BYTE_ARRAY[12] as a signed 96-bit
#     LITTLE-ENDIAN epoch-unit count. This is NOT legacy INT96's Julian layout;
#     it is outside parquet4j's supported timestamp interpretation.
#   9 reserved (no modern INTERVAL alternative).
#  10 INTEGER: bitWidth=8/16/32 uses INT32; bitWidth=64 uses INT64. isSigned
#     controls value interpretation and statistics comparison (unsigned when
#     false), not the physical bytes. Smaller widths constrain the value range.
#  11 UNKNOWN: any physical carrier, all values null.
#  12 JSON: BYTE_ARRAY containing UTF-8 JSON text.
#  13 BSON: BYTE_ARRAY containing BINARY BSON, NOT UTF-8 text.
#  14 UUID: FIXED_LEN_BYTE_ARRAY[16], big-endian UUID bytes (no byte swapping).
# Newer upstream alternatives, NOT in the pinned generated definitions:
#  15 FLOAT16: FIXED_LEN_BYTE_ARRAY[2], IEEE binary16 little-endian.
#  16 VARIANT: annotated group; use VariantEncoding.md, not a single primitive.
#  17 GEOMETRY / 18 GEOGRAPHY: BYTE_ARRAY WKB; use Geospatial.md for CRS/edges.
#  19 FILE: annotated group referencing inline/external bytes; LogicalTypes.md.
# These types require their dedicated semantics; this is not a payload spec
# for them. Their inclusion here must not be read as parquet4j support.
# ConvertedType (UTF8, MAP, MAP_KEY_VALUE, LIST, ENUM, DECIMAL, DATE, TIME_*,
# TIMESTAMP_*, UINT_*, INT_*, JSON, BSON, INTERVAL) is the legacy representation.
# INTERVAL uses FIXED_LEN_BYTE_ARRAY[12] = months, days, milliseconds as three
# unsigned u32 LE components; its order is undefined in current upstream.
#
# RowGroup (field ids):
#   1: required list<ColumnChunk> columns   one per leaf, schema leaf order
#   2: required i64 total_byte_size         uncompressed size (see note below)
#   3: required i64 num_rows                rows in this row group
#   4: optional list<SortingColumn> sorting_columns
#   5: optional i64 file_offset             first page of the row group
#   6: optional i64 total_compressed_size   compressed size (same accounting)
#   7: optional i16 ordinal
#   Note: total_byte_size conventionally includes page headers, i.e. the sum of
#   ColumnMetaData.total_uncompressed_size (which per spec includes headers).
#   The spec text ("uncompressed column data") is ambiguous on headers; the
#   de-facto convention is "including headers" (parquet-mr, this repo).
#
# ColumnChunk (field ids):
#   1: optional string file_path    relative path; current upstream restricts
#                                   usage to "_metadata" summary files
#   2: required i64 file_offset     DEPRECATED - historically pointed at the
#         ColumnMetaData or at the first page, inconsistently across writers;
#         current spec says write 0 unless ColumnMetaData is replicated outside
#         the footer. NEVER use it to find data; use data_page_offset /
#         dictionary_page_offset instead.
#   3: optional ColumnMetaData meta_data   populate for ordinary plaintext chunks;
#                                         encrypted metadata uses field 9
#   4: optional i64 offset_index_offset    page index (OffsetIndex) location
#   5: optional i32 offset_index_length
#   6: optional i64 column_index_offset    page index (ColumnIndex) location
#   7: optional i32 column_index_length
#   8: optional ColumnCryptoMetaData crypto_metadata
#   9: optional binary encrypted_column_metadata
#
# ColumnMetaData (field ids):
#   1: required Type type                     physical type
#   2: required list<Encoding> encodings      every encoding used in the chunk
#   3: required list<string> path_in_schema   leaf path (see SchemaElement rules)
#   4: required CompressionCodec codec        chunk-level codec (section 7)
#   5: required i64 num_values                ALL slots, including nulls
#   6: required i64 total_uncompressed_size   sum of (header + uncompressed body)
#   7: required i64 total_compressed_size     sum of (header + stored body)
#   8: optional list<KeyValue> key_value_metadata
#   9: required i64 data_page_offset          file offset of the first DATA page
#                                             header (V1 or V2)
#  10: optional i64 index_page_offset
#  11: optional i64 dictionary_page_offset    file offset of the dictionary page
#                                             header; must be < data_page_offset
#  12: optional Statistics statistics
#  13: optional list<PageEncodingStats> encoding_stats
#  14: optional i64 bloom_filter_offset
#  15: optional i32 bloom_filter_length         newer than pinned definitions
#  16: optional SizeStatistics size_statistics  newer than pinned definitions
#  17: optional GeospatialStatistics geospatial_statistics (newer; Geospatial.md)
#   Size accounting: total_*_size cover the whole chunk INCLUDING page headers
#   (and the dictionary page if present), not just bodies.
#
# Statistics (field ids) - optional at page and chunk level:
#   1: optional binary max              DEPRECATED bounds, signed order only
#   2: optional binary min              DEPRECATED bounds, signed order only
#   3: optional i64 null_count          distinguish ABSENT from 0!
#   4: optional i64 distinct_count
#   5: optional binary max_value        current bounds (ColumnOrder)
#   6: optional binary min_value        current bounds (ColumnOrder)
#   7: optional bool is_max_value_exact
#   8: optional bool is_min_value_exact
#   9: optional i64 nan_count           FLOAT/DOUBLE/FLOAT16; absent = unknown
#   Fields 7-9 are newer upstream; pinned 2.9.0 Statistics has only fields 1-6.
#   Encoding: bounds are PLAIN-encoded EXCEPT variable-length byte arrays omit
#   the four-byte length prefix (raw bytes only). BOOLEAN is one packed byte;
#   numeric bounds use their fixed physical width. Logical annotations still
#   control interpretation (e.g. DECIMAL bytes stay big-endian).
#   min_value/max_value follow FileMetaData.column_orders, with exactly one
#   order per leaf. There is NO TYPE_ORDER default when column_orders is absent:
#   the modern bounds' order is undefined and cannot justify pruning rows.
#   TYPE_ORDER means logical-type order when annotated, otherwise physical-type
#   order: unsigned lexicographic bytes for raw binary/STRING/UUID; signed ints;
#   unsigned ints for INTEGER(isSigned=false); numeric DECIMAL; false<true.
#   Conservative bounds may be shorter NON-OBSERVED values ("B".."C" bounding
#   "Blart"). The lower bound must be <= every present value; upper >= every
#   present value under that order. Truncation must widen bounds, never exclude
#   actual data. Exactness flags indicate observed extrema; absent != exact.
#   All-null/empty chunks have no min/max; all-NaN TYPE_ORDER pages have none
#   either. An empty binary bound is a valid value, not an absent field.
#   Floating TYPE_ORDER compatibility: ignore NaN bounds; min=+0 may include -0,
#   max=-0 may include +0; min/max cannot answer a search for NaN. Current upstream
#   requires nan_count even when zero for floating TYPE_ORDER or total-order
#   stats; older generated definitions cannot write it. See section 11 [L5].
#   A nested leaf's null_count counts def<max_def slots, NOT top-level null rows.
#   distinct_count is a chunk-wide optional count, never sum(page distincts).
#   Unknown/inconsistent stats are optimization hints to ignore, not grounds
#   for dropping data; do not assume missing null_count or nan_count means 0.
#
# ColumnOrder union alternatives:
#   1 TYPE_ORDER (TypeDefinedOrder)          present in pinned 2.9.0 definitions
#   2 IEEE_754_TOTAL_ORDER (IEEE754TotalOrder)  newer upstream, floating only
#   3 INT96_TIMESTAMP_ORDER (Int96TimestampOrder) newer upstream, INT96 only
# The order controls bounds, NOT whether the column's rows are physically
# sorted (SortingColumn is separate). Unknown order -> ignore ordered bounds.
# Supporting metadata structs (all Compact-encoded):
#   KeyValue: 1 required string key, 2 optional string value (absent != empty).
#   SortingColumn: 1 required i32 column_idx (zero-based LEAF index),
#     2 required bool descending, 3 required bool nulls_first. This declares
#     actual row ordering for that group; it does not sort anything on read.
#   PageEncodingStats: 1 required PageType page_type, 2 required Encoding
#     encoding, 3 required i32 count. Counts pages by type/encoding, not values.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5b. Optional side structures and encrypted-file boundary
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# These are NOT ordinary pages and have no PageHeader. Locate them through
# ColumnChunk/ColumnMetaData offsets and lengths, not by walking chunk pages.
# Their structs are Compact-serialized; validate each referenced byte range.
# They are optional optimizations: a reader without them can scan all pages.
#
# Page index (PageIndex.md):
#   OffsetIndex:
#     1 required list<PageLocation> page_locations (DATA pages only)
#     2 optional list<i64> unencoded_byte_array_data_bytes (newer upstream)
#   PageLocation:
#     1 required i64 offset                 absolute offset of page header
#     2 required i32 compressed_page_size   HEADER + stored body (unlike the
#                                           same name in PageHeader!)
#     3 required i64 first_row_index        zero-based within the row group
#   ColumnIndex:
#     1 required list<bool> null_pages      true iff all values in page are null
#     2 required list<binary> min_values    bounds, same encoding/order as stats
#     3 required list<binary> max_values
#     4 required BoundaryOrder boundary_order (0 UNORDERED, 1 ASCENDING,
#                                               2 DESCENDING)
#     5 optional list<i64> null_counts
#     6/7 optional list<i64> repetition/definition_level_histograms (newer)
#     8 optional list<i64> nan_counts (newer)
#   Corresponding list entries refer to the SAME data page; lengths must agree.
#   null_pages=true uses empty bound entries; those entries are not real bounds.
#   OffsetIndex may exist alone; ColumnIndex requires OffsetIndex. Locations
#   increase by offset and first_row_index. Pages with OffsetIndex must start
#   at row boundaries even for V1. Ordered bounds need not be disjoint:
#   max_values[i] <= min_values[i+1] is NOT guaranteed. Use logical row ranges
#   to skip matching rows in other columns, not their page ordinals.
#
# Bloom filter (BloomFilter.md):
#   bloom_filter_offset -> BloomFilterHeader + bitset (not PageHeader + body).
#   Header fields: 1 required i32 numBytes (BITSET bytes), 2 algorithm union,
#   3 hash union, 4 compression union. Specified alternatives are BLOCK,
#   XXHASH, UNCOMPRESSED (each union field id 1). bloom_filter_length, when
#   present, includes BOTH serialized header and bitset; this field is newer
#   than the pinned 2.9.0 definitions. Without it parse the header for numBytes.
#   Split-block bitsets contain 256-bit blocks (eight 32-bit words). Hash each
#   present physical value using XXH64 seed=0 over its PLAIN bytes, except omit
#   BYTE_ARRAY's length prefix. For block selection and bit masks use the exact
#   algorithm/constants in BloomFilter.md; do not substitute a generic bloom.
#   A negative membership test can exclude a value; a positive test only means
#   MAYBE present. Nulls are not ordinary hashed values. Unknown algorithm,
#   hash, or compression means ignore this optimization, not reject all rows.
#
# SizeStatistics (ColumnMetaData field 16, newer than pinned definitions):
#   1 optional i64 unencoded_byte_array_data_bytes (excluding length prefixes)
#   2 optional list<i64> repetition_level_histogram, one per level 0..max_rep
#   3 optional list<i64> definition_level_histogram, one per level 0..max_def
#   These estimate decoded memory/nesting counts, NOT compressed chunk length.
#
# Modular encryption (Encryption.md; outside this unencrypted grammar):
#   - Encrypted-footer files use "PARE" at BOTH ends. The trailer length covers
#     FileCryptoMetaData plus the encrypted footer; do not Thrift-decode it as
#     a plaintext FileMetaData or merely replace its magic with "PAR1".
#   - Plaintext-footer encrypted files still use "PAR1". Their trailer length
#     covers FileMetaData plus a 28-byte footer signature. Check encryption
#     metadata: PAR1 alone does not prove that every column/page is plaintext.
#   - ColumnCryptoMetaData/encrypted_column_metadata and encrypted modules
#     require keys, authenticated decryption, module framing, and AAD rules.
#     Unsupported encryption must be handled explicitly, never interpreted as
#     ordinary compressed bytes. CRC is not an authentication mechanism.
# parquet4j does not implement these side structures or encryption (section 11).
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 6. Encodings
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# An encoding is named per page (DataPageHeader.encoding / V2 equivalent) for
# the VALUES region only; levels use hybrid RLE except legacy V1 BIT_PACKED.
# Valid combinations (per Encodings.md):
#
#   encoding                 enum  physical types
#   PLAIN                      0   all
#   PLAIN_DICTIONARY           2   all (DEPRECATED; data pages -> RLE_DICTIONARY,
#                                  dictionary pages -> PLAIN)
#   RLE                        3   BOOLEAN values, dictionary indices, levels
#   BIT_PACKED                 4   levels only (DEPRECATED)
#   DELTA_BINARY_PACKED        5   INT32, INT64
#   DELTA_LENGTH_BYTE_ARRAY    6   BYTE_ARRAY
#   DELTA_BYTE_ARRAY           7   BYTE_ARRAY, FIXED_LEN_BYTE_ARRAY
#   RLE_DICTIONARY             8   all (index stream)
#   BYTE_STREAM_SPLIT          9   INT32, INT64, FLOAT, DOUBLE, FIXED_LEN_BYTE_ARRAY
#   ALP                       10   FLOAT, DOUBLE (preview as of 2026)
#
# 6a. PLAIN (0)
plain_encoding: bits =
    """
    PLAIN: values back to back, no metadata.

      BOOLEAN:              bit-packed, 8 values per byte, LSB first
                            (value i is bit (i mod 8) of byte (i div 8));
                            the last byte is zero-padded
      INT32:                4 bytes little-endian
      INT64:                8 bytes little-endian
      INT96:                12 bytes little-endian (deprecated). In practice
                            the legacy timestamp layout is an 8-byte
                            little-endian nanos-of-day followed by a 4-byte
                            little-endian Julian day; treat as opaque 12 bytes.
      FLOAT:                4 bytes IEEE-754 little-endian
      DOUBLE:               8 bytes IEEE-754 little-endian
      BYTE_ARRAY:           u32 LE byte length, then the bytes
      FIXED_LEN_BYTE_ARRAY: type_length raw bytes
    """;

# 6b. Dictionary encoding (PLAIN_DICTIONARY = 2 deprecated, RLE_DICTIONARY = 8)
dictionary_encoding: bits =
    """
    A column chunk may carry ONE optional dictionary page (always the first
    page of the chunk), whose body holds num_values entries in PLAIN encoding.

    A dictionary belongs to ONE leaf in ONE row group; reset it on entering
    the next chunk. Never infer dictionary use solely from its presence: later
    data pages can use PLAIN or another supported non-dictionary encoding.
    Dictionary-encoded data pages store INDICES into that dictionary:

      index stream = u8 bit_width          (single byte, 0..32 - NOT a varint)
                 &   rle-bit-packed-hybrid (indices at that bit width)

    The index stream has NO 4-byte length prefix in either page version; it
    simply runs to the end of the values region. bit_width 0 is legal (a
    one-entry dictionary: all indices are 0). If the dictionary gets too large,
    writers fall back to PLAIN for the remaining data pages of the chunk
    (ColumnMetaData.encodings then lists both). Decode only present-value
    indices, not null slots; every actual index must be < dictionary entry count.
    """;

# 6c. RLE / bit-packed hybrid (RLE = 3)
# Used ONLY for: repetition/definition levels, dictionary indices, BOOLEAN
# values (as an alternative to PLAIN).
rle_bitpacked_hybrid: bits =
    """
    RLE/Bit-Packing Hybrid Encoding.

    stream = run*
    run    = bit-packed run | repeated (RLE) run

    Run headers are ULEB-128 varints (UNSIGNED - not zigzag) whose least
    significant bit selects the run type:

      repeated run    header = count << 1            (LSB = 0)
                      payload = the value, fixed width
                                round-up-to-next-byte(bit_width), little-endian
                      the value repeats `count` times

      bit-packed run  header = (groups << 1) | 1     (LSB = 1)
                      groups = run length / 8 (values are packed 8 at a time)
                      payload = groups * bit_width bytes

    NOTE (frequently got wrong): LSB 0 = repeated/RLE run, LSB 1 = bit-packed
    run. (An older revision of this document had the polarity inverted.)

    Bit-packing is an LSB-first bit stream. Bit b of value i occupies overall
    bit position i*w+b: byte floor((i*w+b)/8), bit ((i*w+b) mod 8), for 0<=b<w.
    Values may cross byte boundaries. This arithmetic removes the ambiguity of
    diagrams that print each byte MSB-first. For w=3, values 0,1,2,3,4,5,6,7
    produce payload 88 C6 FA (hex); a one-group run is 03 88 C6 FA including
    its run header. Deprecated BIT_PACKED packs MSB-first instead (6h).

    Repeated value uses ceil(w/8) bytes; unused high bits must be zero. At
    width 0 there are ZERO value bytes: a repeated count still comes from the
    header, all decoded values are zero. Do not read a phantom payload byte.

    Constraints: run lengths (both kinds) must be in [1, 2^31 - 1]. Zero-length
    runs are invalid. The final bit-packed run may carry fewer real values than
    groups * 8; the tail (at most 7 values) is padding. An empty stream
    represents zero values.

    Length-prefix framing (when the hybrid stream is preceded by a 4-byte
    little-endian byte length):

      +--------------+-------------------+-----------------+
      | Page kind    | stream            | 4-byte length?  |
      +--------------+-------------------+-----------------+
      | Data page v1 | definition levels | yes             |
      |              | repetition levels | yes             |
      |              | dictionary indices| no              |
      |              | BOOLEAN values    | yes             |
      +--------------+-------------------+-----------------+
      | Data page v2 | definition levels | no (header len) |
      |              | repetition levels | no (header len) |
      |              | dictionary indices| no              |
      |              | BOOLEAN values    | yes             |
      +--------------+-------------------+-----------------+

    BOOLEAN values with encoding RLE (3): 4-byte LE length, then a hybrid
    stream at bit width 1 (0 = false, 1 = true) of the PRESENT values only.
    """;

# 6d. DELTA_BINARY_PACKED (5) - INT32 and INT64
delta_encoding: bits =
    """
    Delta Binary Packed Encoding (DELTA_BINARY_PACKED).

    Adapted from Lemire & Boytsov, "Decoding billions of integers per second
    through vectorization". Signed values use zigzag + ULEB-128; unsigned
    quantities (counts, sizes) use plain ULEB-128.

    stream = header & block*
    header = <block size in values>          ULEB-128, multiple of 128
           & <miniblocks per block>          ULEB-128; values per miniblock =
                                             block size / miniblocks per block
                                             must be a multiple of 32
           & <total value count>             ULEB-128
           & <first value>                   zigzag ULEB-128 (of the type width)

    block  = <min delta>                     zigzag ULEB-128 (frame of reference
                                             for THIS block, not per miniblock)
           & <bit widths>                    one raw byte per miniblock (the
                                             whole table is always present,
                                             even for unused miniblocks)
           & <miniblock>*                    only the used miniblocks

    miniblock = bit-packed (value - min delta) deltas at the miniblock's width,
                padded to full size: values_per_miniblock * width / 8 bytes.

    Reconstruction:
      values[0] = first value (header)
      each subsequent value = previous value + min_delta + unpacked delta bits
    Arithmetic wraps in two's complement on overflow (intentional).

    Padding rules: the last miniblock is padded to full byte length (pad bits
    should be zero but readers must accept any bits); unused miniblock width
    bytes in the last block should be zero but readers must accept any value,
    and those miniblocks have NO payload bytes. The reader counts values
    (total value count) to know where to stop - do not use widths to delimit.

    Mini-block bits use the LSB-first packing of 6c, WITHOUT hybrid run
    headers. Width <= 32 for INT32 and <= 64 for INT64; zero width needs no
    payload. The total count is the PRESENT-value count, not the slot count.
    Across blocks retain the previous value, but restart encoding at each page.

    Valid byte example: values 7,5,3,1,2,3,4,5; block=128, miniblocks=4,
    32 deltas per miniblock. Deltas -2,-2,-2,1,1,1,1; relative 0,0,0,3,3,3,3.
      header: 80 01 04 08 0E   (128,4,8,zigzag(7))
      block:  03 02 00 00 00   (zigzag(-2), widths [2,0,0,0])
      used miniblock payload: C0 3F 00 00 00 00 00 00
    Only seven deltas are real; the other 25 slots of that miniblock are
    zero padding. The three unused miniblocks have no payload bytes.
    """;

# 6e. DELTA_LENGTH_BYTE_ARRAY (6) - BYTE_ARRAY
delta_length_encoding: bits =
    """
    Delta-length byte array: all lengths first, then all bytes.

      stream = <DELTA_BINARY_PACKED INT32 lengths> & <bytes...>

    Decode exactly the present-value count of INT32 lengths; reject negative
    lengths and overflow in their sum. Consume the COMPLETE padded delta
    miniblock before locating the byte region, not just the real deltas.
    The sum of lengths must match the remaining byte payload.
    The value bytes are the plain concatenation of the arrays in order (no
    length prefixes inside the byte region). Example: "Hello", "World",
    "Foobar", "ABCDEF" -> DeltaEncoding(5, 5, 6, 6) + "HelloWorldFoobarABCDEF".
    """;

# 6f. DELTA_BYTE_ARRAY (7) - BYTE_ARRAY, FIXED_LEN_BYTE_ARRAY
delta_byte_array_encoding: bits =
    """
    Delta byte array (incremental / front compression): each value shares a
    prefix with the previous value; store the prefix length and the suffix.

      stream = <DELTA_BINARY_PACKED INT32 prefix lengths>
             & <DELTA_BINARY_PACKED INT32 suffix lengths>
             & <suffix bytes concatenated>

    (I.e. the suffixes are exactly a DELTA_LENGTH_BYTE_ARRAY stream; the prefix
    lengths are one more DELTA_BINARY_PACKED stream in front.)

    Both count headers must equal the present-value count; prefix and suffix
    lengths must be nonnegative and reconstructed lengths must fit bounds.
    Prefix history resets for each page (older buggy writers sometimes carried
    history across pages; any compatibility workaround must be writer-specific).
    value[0] prefix must be 0; value[i] prefix cannot exceed len(value[i-1]);
    value[i] = value[i-1][0..prefix) + suffix. For FIXED_LEN_BYTE_ARRAY all
    lengths are still encoded despite the fixed width.

    Example: "axis", "axle", "babble", "babyhood"
      prefixes 0, 2, 0, 3 ; suffix lengths 4, 2, 6, 5 ; bytes "axislebabbleyhood"
    """;

# 6g. BYTE_STREAM_SPLIT (9) - FLOAT, DOUBLE, INT32, INT64, FIXED_LEN_BYTE_ARRAY
byte_stream_split_encoding: bits =
    """
    Byte Stream Split: de-interleave the bytes of N fixed-width values into K
    byte planes (K = value width in bytes), concatenated plane by plane.

      stream = plane 0 (N bytes) & plane 1 & ... & plane K-1

    Byte j of value i lives at plane j, offset i. The total is exactly K * N
    bytes - no metadata, no padding; the planes run to the end of the values
    region. Does not shrink data by itself but greatly improves subsequent
    compression on float-like data.

    Example (three 32-bit values with bytes AA BB CC DD | 00 11 22 33 | A3 B4 C5 D6):
      planes: AA 00 A3 | BB 11 B4 | CC 22 C5 | DD 33 D6
    """;

# 6h. BIT_PACKED (4) - DEPRECATED, levels only
bit_packed_encoding: bits =
    """
    Deprecated bit-packed encoding (BIT_PACKED = 4): fixed-width values packed
    back to back WITHOUT run headers, padded to whole bytes. WARNING: it packs
    from the MOST significant bit of each byte downward - the opposite order of
    the hybrid encoding's bit-packed runs. Only ever used for (old) level
    streams; superseded by RLE = 3. V1 BIT_PACKED level sections have neither
    run headers NOR a byte-length prefix; use ceil(num_values*width/8) bytes.
    For width=3 and values 0..7 the payload is 05 39 77, not hybrid 88 C6 FA.
    V2 always uses hybrid RLE and cannot select this legacy layout.
    """;

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 7. Compression
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CompressionCodec (chunk-level, ColumnMetaData.codec):
#   UNCOMPRESSED (0), SNAPPY (1), GZIP (2), LZO (3), BROTLI (4), LZ4 (5,
#   DEPRECATED - Hadoop-frame based), ZSTD (6), LZ4_RAW (7).
# Exact framing per codec: see Compression.md (e.g. GZIP members may be
# concatenated; SNAPPY is the raw block format; LZ4 vs LZ4_RAW differ in
# framing; ZSTD is a standard frame).
#
# Where compression applies:
#   - V1 pages: the ENTIRE body (levels + values) is one compressed unit.
#     All pages of a chunk use the chunk codec uniformly.
#   - V2 pages: ONLY the values region; level sections are always raw. The
#     per-page flag is_compressed selects it (omitted = TRUE):
#       is_compressed true/omitted: values region compressed with the chunk
#         codec; uncompressed_page_size = level bytes + decoded values bytes
#       is_compressed explicitly false: values region stored raw and
#         compressed_page_size == uncompressed_page_size
#     This permits MIXED compression inside one chunk (some pages compressed,
#     some raw) - legal for V2, impossible for V1. A chunk may still declare a
#     non-UNCOMPRESSED codec even if every V2 data page opts out. parquet4j
#     chooses UNCOMPRESSED when none retain compression; this is writer policy,
#     NOT a format requirement. Dictionary pages always use the chunk codec
#     over their whole body, regardless of any data-page is_compressed flags.
#     is_compressed=true with codec=UNCOMPRESSED is a legal no-op.
#
# Decoder side: always know the expected decoded size BEFORE decompressing
# (V1: uncompressed_page_size; V2: uncompressed_page_size minus the level
# lengths) and reject mismatches/oversized output.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 8. Thrift Compact Protocol (used for FileMetaData and every page header)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
thrift_compact_protocol: bits =
    """
    Apache Thrift Compact Protocol binary serialization
    (https://github.com/apache/thrift/blob/master/doc/specs/thrift-compact-protocol.md).

    Type codes (low nibble of field headers):
      0 STOP            5 I32              10 SET
      1 BOOLEAN_TRUE    6 I64              11 MAP
      2 BOOLEAN_FALSE   7 DOUBLE           12 STRUCT
      3 BYTE            8 BINARY
      4 I16             9 LIST

    Field header (struct fields):
      delta in 1..15:   one byte = (delta << 4) | type   (delta = id - prev id)
      otherwise:        one byte = type, then field id as zigzag ULEB-128 i16
      end of struct:    one STOP byte 0x00
      Track previous field id PER STRUCT, initialized to 0; push/pop this
      state for nested structs. A STOP inside a nested struct is NOT the end
      of the page header; never search raw bytes for the first 0x00.
      A bool field's VALUE is encoded in the header type itself
      (1 = true, 2 = false) - no payload byte follows.

    Values:
      I16/I32/I64:  zigzag then ULEB-128 varint.
                    zigzag(n) = (n << 1) ^ (n >> 31)   [i32]
                              = (n << 1) ^ (n >> 63)   [i64]
                    decode: (u >>> 1) ^ -(u & 1)
      BYTE:         one raw byte
      DOUBLE:       8 bytes, little-endian IEEE-754
      BINARY:       ULEB-128 byte length, then the bytes (strings are UTF-8)

    LIST/SET header:
      size 0..14:   one byte = (size << 4) | element_type
      size > 14:    one byte = 0xF0 | element_type, then ULEB-128 size
      then `size` elements. Collection BOOL has element type 1; each VALUE
      is its own byte: 0x01=true, 0x02=false (NOT 0x00=false). Same for map bools.

    MAP header:
      empty map:    one byte 0x00 (no type byte follows)
      non-empty:    ULEB-128 size, then one byte = (key_type << 4) | value_type,
                    then `size` key/value pairs

    STRUCT:         fields as above until STOP. Unknown fields MUST be skipped
                    by their type (forward compatibility); enum values travel
                    as I32. Note ULEB-128 (unsigned) appears for lengths/sizes
                    and zigzag-ULEB128 for signed ints - do not mix them up.
    """;

# ULEB-128 ("varint") as used by RLE headers, delta headers, and Thrift lengths
varint: bits =
    """
    Variable-length integer encoding (ULEB-128, unsigned).

    Each byte contains 7 bits of data (bits 0-6) and a continuation bit (bit 7):
    - If bit 7 = 1, more bytes follow
    - If bit 7 = 0, this is the last byte

    The 7-bit data chunks are concatenated little-endian-first (the first byte
    carries the least significant bits) to form the integer.

    Example: 300 (0x012C)
      Binary: 00000001 00101100
      Varint: 10101100 00000010
              ^------^ ^-------
              0x2C      0x02
              (cont=1)  (cont=0)

    SIGNED integers (Thrift I16/I32/I64, delta first_value/min_delta) are
    zigzag-mapped to unsigned BEFORE this encoding: zigzag(n) = (n << 1) ^
    (n >> 31 or 63). RLE run headers and length/size fields are unsigned and
    must NOT be zigzagged.
    """;

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 9. Enumerations
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# These are the Thrift enum values from org.apache.parquet.format
# (parquet.thrift). io.github.aloksingh.parquet.model mirrors physical types,
# codecs, and encodings through BYTE_STREAM_SPLIT; ALP below is upstream-only.

# PageType (parquet.thrift PageType)
PAGE_TYPE_DATA_PAGE       = 0;
PAGE_TYPE_INDEX_PAGE      = 1;
PAGE_TYPE_DICTIONARY_PAGE = 2;
PAGE_TYPE_DATA_PAGE_V2    = 3;

# Physical types (parquet.thrift Type)
TYPE_BOOLEAN              = 0;
TYPE_INT32                = 1;
TYPE_INT64                = 2;
TYPE_INT96                = 3;  # Deprecated - used for legacy nanosecond timestamps
TYPE_FLOAT                = 4;
TYPE_DOUBLE               = 5;
TYPE_BYTE_ARRAY           = 6;
TYPE_FIXED_LEN_BYTE_ARRAY = 7;

# Encodings (parquet.thrift Encoding; value 1 = reserved, never used)
ENCODING_PLAIN                 = 0;
# (1 reserved: GROUP_VAR_INT, never used)
ENCODING_PLAIN_DICTIONARY      = 2;  # Deprecated
ENCODING_RLE                   = 3;
ENCODING_BIT_PACKED            = 4;  # Deprecated
ENCODING_DELTA_BINARY_PACKED   = 5;
ENCODING_DELTA_LENGTH_BYTE_ARRAY = 6;
ENCODING_DELTA_BYTE_ARRAY      = 7;
ENCODING_RLE_DICTIONARY        = 8;
ENCODING_BYTE_STREAM_SPLIT     = 9;
ENCODING_ALP                   = 10; # Preview (2026); not implemented here

# Compression codecs (parquet.thrift CompressionCodec)
CODEC_UNCOMPRESSED = 0;
CODEC_SNAPPY       = 1;
CODEC_GZIP         = 2;
CODEC_LZO          = 3;
CODEC_BROTLI       = 4;
CODEC_LZ4          = 5;  # Deprecated (Hadoop-frame LZ4)
CODEC_ZSTD         = 6;
CODEC_LZ4_RAW      = 7;

# Field repetition types (parquet.thrift FieldRepetitionType)
REPETITION_REQUIRED = 0;  # Field is required (appears exactly once)
REPETITION_OPTIONAL = 1;  # Field is optional (appears 0 or 1 times)
REPETITION_REPEATED = 2;  # Field is repeated (appears 0 or more times)

# ==============================================================================
# 10. Implementation notes - this repository (parquet4j)
# ==============================================================================
#
# Source paths below are relative to src/main/java/io/github/aloksingh/parquet/;
# test names refer to src/test/java/io/github/aloksingh/parquet/. Re-audit these
# contracts when implementation changes. Support differs by API layer.
#
# 10a. Writer: ParquetFileWriter + writer/*
#   - Writable schemas: required/optional top-level primitives and canonical
#     top-level MAP with required primitive key and required/optional primitive
#     value. MAP container may be required/optional. LIST, STRUCT, nested MAP
#     values, arbitrary nested paths, repeated primitives, and INT96 are rejected
#     by writer/WriterSchema.java:25-78,104-122. Format support is broader.
#   - WriterFileSchema emits descriptor annotations plus their legacy fields;
#     MAP emits modern and converted MAP. Raw BYTE_ARRAY is not automatically
#     STRING. Callers supply PHYSICAL values: logical dates/decimals/UUIDs need
#     explicit PrimitiveLogicalType.toPhysicalValue conversion before writing
#     (writer/WriterValues.java; model/PrimitiveLogicalType.java:446-529).
#   - V2-only, PLAIN values, no dictionary. WriterLevelBuffer emits unprefixed
#     repeated hybrid runs, omitting zero-max-level streams. One row is never
#     split across pages; leaf columns can split at different row boundaries.
#     Null/empty MAP placeholders are null leaf slots, including for keys.
#   - Page/group soft targets default to 1 MiB/128 MiB, measured as uncompressed
#     encoded payload, excluding headers/footer/statistics; NOT compressed size
#     or row count. One complete row may exceed a soft target; hard body/event
#     limits still apply. Encoded pages buffer in memory until group flush
#     (ParquetFileWriter.java:88-89,277-285; writer/WriterColumnBuilder.java).
#   - ColumnMetaData.encodings is the fixed [RLE, PLAIN] pair, overinclusive
#     when a required flat column has no level stream. Dictionary offset absent.
#   - Compression retention tests VALUES bytes only: candidate_size/raw_size
#     must be STRICTLY LESS than minCompressionRatio (default 0.90). Exactly
#     10% savings is rejected. Threshold 0 disables compression. Flag is always
#     explicit; chunk codec is configured codec if any page retained compression,
#     otherwise UNCOMPRESSED (ParquetFileWriter.java:374-414).
#   - Both codec factories implement UNCOMPRESSED, SNAPPY, GZIP, ZSTD, legacy
#     Hadoop-framed LZ4, LZ4_RAW. LZO/BROTLI are enum values, NOT implementations.
#     GZIP reader accepts concatenated members; LZ4_RAW is not LZ4 framing.
#   - Statistics on pages/chunks: null_count always; distinct_count exact or
#     omitted after >4096 distinct values or >1 MiB tracked. Bounds only when
#     non-null, non-NaN values exist and the logical order is known. NaNs
#     excluded; min zero=-0, max zero=+0. Bounds honor descriptor annotations:
#     INTEGER(isSigned=false) uses unsigned integer order; binary DECIMAL uses
#     signed big-endian unscaled numeric order. Raw binary/STRING/UUID retain
#     unsigned byte-wise order. Physical value/bound encodings do not change.
#     Deprecated min/max are omitted for unsigned INTEGER and all binary types.
#     Unknown annotation order omits bounds but retains counts. Row/page/chunk
#     merges require equal physical type/width and logical annotations before
#     changing statistics; see the fixed [L6] finding.
#   - Footer: version=1; root + DFS schema nodes; TYPE_ORDER per leaf;
#     created_by="java-parquet-rs ParquetFileWriter" (actual legacy string, not
#     this project's name/version); no user key_value_metadata. RowGroup sizes
#     include headers; ColumnChunk.file_offset uses legacy first-page address
#     rather than current upstream's recommendation of 0 (see [A2]).
#   - First accepted row/start creates a sibling .parquet4j-*.tmp. Successful
#     close flushes chunks, writes footer/trailer, closes the sink, then uses
#     ATOMIC_MOVE + REPLACE_EXISTING with no non-atomic fallback. Empty close
#     emits a zero-row file. Failure aborts the whole writer and attempts temp
#     cleanup. Atomic publication is NOT an fsync/durability guarantee
#     (ParquetFileWriter.java:188-237,422-487; WriterTransactionalTest).
#
# 10b. Physical reader: PageReader + model/ColumnPageDecoder
#   - Reads V1/V2 and dictionary pages. PLAIN covers all physical types,
#     including raw INT96. Dictionary data encodings 2/8 and dictionary-page
#     PLAIN/legacy PLAIN_DICTIONARY are accepted; one dictionary per chunk,
#     installed before data, with index/count checks. ALP is rejected.
#   - Values: DELTA_BINARY_PACKED for INT32/INT64; DELTA_LENGTH_BYTE_ARRAY for
#     BYTE_ARRAY; DELTA_BYTE_ARRAY for BYTE_ARRAY/FIXED_LEN_BYTE_ARRAY;
#     BYTE_STREAM_SPLIT for INT32/INT64/FLOAT/DOUBLE/FIXED_LEN_BYTE_ARRAY;
#     RLE values for BOOLEAN. Invalid combinations fail rather than yield nulls
#     (model/ColumnPageDecoder.java:28-138,146-174).
#   - Levels: hybrid-RLE only. Enforces level maxima, exact decoded slot counts,
#     full run payloads, permitted final padding, no trailing bytes. Explicit
#     width-zero V2 streams accepted. V1 legacy selectors ignored: [L1].
#   - Chunk metadata checks: nonnegative offsets/sizes/counts, dictionary order,
#     source length and overflow, first data offset within declared end. Every
#     page header/body is bounded by that declared end; this is NOT a full
#     footer/chunk/accounting validation (PageReader.java:64-95,149-169; [L4]).
#   - Direct PageReader limits default to header 1 MiB, stored body 64 MiB,
#     decoded body 128 MiB, 16 Mi values/page. PageReadOptions.STRICT verifies
#     a PRESENT CRC; missing CRC remains allowed. Normal scans use defaults;
#     ReadOptions integrity limits are not wired through: see [L8].
#   - V2: checks level lengths against stored/decoded sizes, false compression
#     flag requires equal sizes, definition-derived null count, 0<=rows<=slots,
#     and rows==slots for flat columns. Repeated row-start counts/boundaries
#     are NOT checked (model/ColumnPageDecoder.java:76-88; [L4]).
#   - INDEX_PAGE/unhandled page types rejected, not skipped. Matching subheader
#     must exist, but extra subheaders are not rejected (PageReader.java:288-321).
#
# 10c. Metadata and logical row APIs (not full format conformance)
#   - Normal ParquetFileReader validates trailing PAR1 and a positive signed
#     footer length; leading magic is only checked by the separate verifyMagic
#     utility. Footer/schema validation is partial: see [L7]/[L10].
#   - Primitive annotations convert row values; physical ColumnValues adapters
#     retain physical bytes/numbers. INT96 is available through decodeAsInt96,
#     but accessing a present INT96 through the row API explicitly rejects it.
#   - SchemaDescriptor reconstructs groups. User-facing columns flatten ordinary
#     STRUCT/LIST to dotted leaf names; repeated leaf rows expose a flattened
#     List, and absent AND empty containers surface as null. MAPs outside
#     repeated ancestors collapse to one Map-valued column; their nested values
#     can be recursively materialized (model/SchemaDescriptor.java:208-239;
#     ParquetRowIterator.java:513-675). This is not a lossless nested LIST API.
#   - NestedStructureReader helpers differ: readMap flattens nested entries and
#     rejects duplicates that flattening would lose; readStruct is a positional
#     leaf zip, not arbitrary nested reconstruction, and treats BYTE_ARRAY as
#     UTF-8 regardless of annotation (NestedStructureReader.java:64-174).
#
# Regression evidence to consult (not a blanket conformance certification):
#   WriterIndependentTest: DuckDB reads writer primitive/MAP output; pages,
#     null slots, whole oversized rows, independent leaf boundaries checked.
#   PageReaderMetadataTest / PageReaderV2SafetyTest / PageReaderChecksumTest:
#     range, V2 size/default-flag, and stored-body CRC assertions.
#   ColumnPageDecoderValidationTest / RleDecoderSafetyTest / CodecLz4FramingTest:
#     malformed framing, value counts, and legacy-versus-raw codec bytes.
#   WriterLogicalStatisticsTest: unsigned/DECIMAL serialized page/footer bounds,
#     physical payload bytes, page/group boundaries, legacy bound omission,
#     annotation-compatible merges, and independent DuckDB filtered reads.
#   LogicalAnnotationReadPathTest / MapAssemblyContractTest:
#     API-level logical conversion/nesting contracts, including their limits.
#   The section 3e/6c/6d byte examples were exercised against the decoder and
#     generated Compact structs, not inferred from a local writer round trip.
#
# ==============================================================================
# 11. Inconsistencies and ambiguities
# ==============================================================================
# [F] corrected DOCUMENTATION; [A] upstream/legacy interpretation; [L]
# IMPLEMENTATION findings. Entries explicitly marked FIXED record code remedies;
# the other [L] entries remain open.
#
# Historical corrections retained from earlier revisions:
#   [F1] Hybrid polarity: 0=repeated, 1=bit-packed (6c).
#   [F2] Delta header has FOUR fields; min_delta is per block (6d).
#   [F3] Page ids: DATA=0, INDEX=1, DICTIONARY=2, V2=3 (3/9).
#   [F4] Empty files allowed; V2 included in data-page alternatives.
#   [F5] Correct Java model package is io.github.aloksingh.parquet.model.
#   [F6] RLE repeated payload is ceil(width/8) bytes; V2 body order/default set.
# Additional corrections in this revision:
#   [F7] Repeated primitive cannot contain null elements. Replaced the invalid
#     phone example with a real three-level nullable-element LIST (4a).
#   [F8] BSON is binary, not UTF-8; DECIMAL integer carriers are LE, only binary
#     decimal carriers are BE; TIME/TIMESTAMP carriers distinguished (5a).
#   [F9] No default ColumnOrder for modern bounds; conservative truncation widens
#     bounds. Compact collection bool false is 02, not 00 (5a/8).
#  [F10] Footer must not overlap initial magic. V1 byte counts now repeat ONE
#     byte, not an arbitrary byte stream; file payload allows side structures.
#     Grammar is explicitly a scaffold, not a standalone executable parser.
#  [F11] ALP acceptance and broad reader validation claims were false; section
#     10 now describes actual API paths and checks. Pinned 2.9.0 definitions
#     are distinguished from later upstream fields/types/orders.
#  [F12] Writer bounds are not always present; compression ratio comparison is
#     strict and values-only; UNCOMPRESSED for all-raw V2 chunks is policy.
#
# Upstream/legacy interpretation (not every pitfall is a spec ambiguity):
#   [A1] File version 2 has no agreed separate meaning in current upstream;
#     write 1, accept 1/2. Page version is independent (parquet.thrift).
#   [A2] ColumnChunk.file_offset historically meant metadata OR first page;
#     current upstream deprecates it and recommends 0 without external metadata.
#     parquet4j writes first page (ParquetFileWriter.java:346-348) and ignores it
#     on read. Seek via the page offsets, never this deprecated field.
#   [A3] RowGroup.total_byte_size wording does not explicitly settle headers;
#     parquet4j includes them, matching sum(column.total_uncompressed_size).
#     ColumnMetaData size inclusion of headers IS explicit, not ambiguous.
#   [A4] Floating TYPE_ORDER has legacy NaN/signed-zero ambiguity. Current
#     upstream supplies compatibility rules and newer IEEE_754_TOTAL_ORDER;
#     old files lacking nan_count cannot prove NaN absence (5a, [L5]).
#   [A5] V1 BIT_PACKED framing IS specified (3b/6h), not an excuse to ignore
#     the declared encoding. The incompatibility is parquet4j's [L1].
#   [A6] No blanket prohibition on mixing V1/V2 pages is stated upstream.
#     Per-page compression rules still apply; independent implementations
#     should be tested before depending on mixed-version chunks.
#   [A7] Omitted V2 is_compressed=true and BOOLEAN statistics use one byte;
#     these are explicit wire rules, NOT unresolved ambiguities (3a/5a).
#
# Implementation findings (only [L6] is fixed; other limitations remain open):
#   [L1] LEGACY LEVELS: PageReader.java:233-252 discards V1 level encoding fields
#     and assumes length-prefixed hybrid RLE. BIT_PACKED can misdecode or fail;
#     the safe future behavior is implement it or explicitly reject it.
#   [L2] WRITER SUBSET: only schemas in 10a, V2/PLAIN, no dictionary/V1/INT96,
#     page indexes, blooms, SizeStatistics, encryption. Fixed [RLE,PLAIN] can
#     advertise unused RLE. Do not infer these capabilities from enum tables.
#   [L3] NEW/OPTIONAL FEATURES: neither the internal enum nor pinned generated
#     enum accepts ALP=10 (model/Encoding.java:65-76). New logical alternatives,
#     bounds orders, and metadata fields are not interpreted. Side indexes,
#     blooms, file_path/encryption metadata are ignored by the adapter; encrypted
#     metadata/pages have no usable decode path. LZO/BROTLI factories reject.
#   [L4] ACCOUNTING: PageReader enforces source/chunk boundaries, but does not
#     reconcile summed page slots/uncompressed sizes, first DATA address,
#     footer/other-chunk overlap, or rep=0 counts with repeated V2 num_rows.
#     Wrong total_compressed_size ending on a page boundary can hide pages;
#     wrong group num_rows can leave unread events. Partial validation is not
#     proof of full-file integrity (PageReader.java:64-95,142-169;
#     model/ColumnPageDecoder.java:82-88; ParquetRowIterator.java:305-327).
#   [L5] NEW FLOAT STATS: pinned Statistics has no nan_count or exactness flags;
#     pinned ColumnOrder has only TYPE_ORDER. Writer excludes NaNs/normalizes
#     zeros but cannot satisfy newer mandatory floating nan_count rules without
#     updated definitions. Older 2.9.0 compatibility != latest-spec completeness.
#   [L6] FIXED - INCORRECT LOGICAL BOUNDS: previously WriterStatistics received
#     only physical Type, losing unsigned INTEGER and binary DECIMAL ordering.
#     UINT32 raw [-1,1] now emits logical min=1,max=4294967295; DECIMAL FIXED[1]
#     precision=2,scale=0 bytes [FF,01] now emit min=-1,max=1. UINT64 and variable
#     binary DECIMAL use the same annotation-aware comparison policy, selected
#     once per statistics instance. Bounds keep their original physical bytes;
#     footer TYPE_ORDER remains unchanged. Deprecated unsigned signed-order
#     bounds are omitted. Unknown order suppresses bounds, not counts; merges
#     reject unequal physical type/width or logical annotations before mutation.
#     writer/WriterStatistics.java:35-51,90-132,173-205;
#     writer/WriterColumnBuffer.java:31; writer/WriterColumnBuilder.java:34.
#     WriterLogicalStatisticsTest checks pages/footer/payload, multi-page/group
#     behavior and independent DuckDB reads. This does NOT fix reader [L11],
#     and files already written with incorrect bounds must be rewritten or read
#     without trusting those bounds for pruning.
#   [L7] FOOTER VALIDATION: normal constructors never call verifyMagic;
#     reproduced acceptance of a valid file after replacing leading PAR1 with
#     BAD!. Signed footer length bound is fileLen-8, allowing metadataStart<4;
#     no explicit configurable footer resource policy or full-byte-consumption
#     check. version is copied without an allowlist:
#     ParquetMetadataReader.java:51-106,145-150; ParquetFileReader.java:63-96.
#     Recommended checks are in sections 5/12.
#   [L8] POLICY PLUMBING: direct PageReader accepts STRICT/custom limits, but
#     RowGroupReader constructs a default PageReader. ReadOptions checksum/page
#     limits and logicalTypes flag are not forwarded/consulted on the normal
#     row path. Do not promise opt-in scan CRC from that API:
#     ParquetFileReader.java:264-277; ParquetRowIterator.java:513-528;
#     ReadOptions.java:80-108.
#   [L9] NESTED SEMANTICS: flattened LIST/STRUCT leaves lose container identity;
#     empty/absent repeated leaf containers both become null. Legacy singleton
#     repeated-group LIST rules for names array/<name>_tuple or repeated child
#     are not fully honored by model/SchemaDescriptor.java:362-389. Modern versus
#     converted group annotations are ORed and LIST tried first, so conflicts
#     can choose legacy LIST over modern MAP (model/SchemaDescriptor.java:286-315).
#     See API limits in 10c.
#  [L10] SCHEMA/METADATA CHECKS: root constraints/primitive num_children are not
#     fully validated; missing non-root repetition defaults to REQUIRED. Column
#     paths/types/counts are not cross-checked against schema leaves, and file
#     row totals/group sizes are not reconciled. Malformed LIST/MAP can fall
#     back to STRUCT (model/SchemaDescriptor.java:184-205,242-315;
#     ParquetMetadataReader.java:125-230). These checks remain implementer work.
#  [L11] PRUNING ORDER: metadata adapter discards column_orders and whether bounds
#     came from modern versus legacy fields, preferring modern then legacy.
#     PredicateStatistics assumes signed primitive order; it cannot establish
#     that external modern bounds have a known/supported order. Unknown/absent
#     orders or incompatible annotations must NOT justify pruning:
#     ParquetMetadataReader.java:174-204; util/filter/PredicateStatistics.java:17-60.
#     Binary
#     pruning is already refused; floating ordered predicates are conservative.
#  [L12] ROW ANNOTATION MATCH: WriterSchema.sameColumn compares physical shape,
#     not PrimitiveLogicalType, so an annotation-only row-schema mismatch can
#     pass validation (writer/WriterSchema.java:81-101,130-135). Conversion and
#     annotation consistency must be enforced separately by future implementers.
#
# Sibling-document drift (out of scope for this edit): docs/PARQUET_FORMAT.md
# still names src/main/java/org/parquet/, describes file_offset as chunk address,
# and implies generator-ready grammar. This Dogma file/upstream references take
# precedence for wire details; the sibling needs its own reconciliation.
#
# ==============================================================================
# 12. Implementer validation checklist
# ==============================================================================
# A reader should distinguish corruption, unsupported features, and ignorable
# unknown optional metadata; none should become successful-looking partial EOF.
# Open [L] findings above identify checks this repository still lacks.
#   1. Validate both magic strings (unencrypted scope), footer range/allocation
#      limit, bounded
#      Compact fields/collections/recursion, required fields, and full metadata
#      consumption. Skip unknown OPTIONAL fields by type; fail unsupported
#      required codecs/encodings/orders appropriately (ignore unknown bounds).
#   2. Rebuild/validate DFS schema, logical carriers and LIST/MAP shape; match
#      each row-group chunk to its leaf path/type/order. Check nonnegative row
#      counts and file/group totals, including empty file/chunk edge cases.
#   3. Bound chunks before footer, reject impossible offsets/overflows/overlap;
#      honor dictionary/data/index offsets without seeking via file_offset.
#      Track actual header+body bytes and reconcile totals, not just EOF.
#   4. Limit header/body/count/decompression allocations before reading; verify
#      matching subheader and exactly sized decompression. V2 level bytes are
#      NOT compressed. If policy verifies CRC, use stored body bytes first.
#   5. Decode each level section using declared V1 framing or raw V2 framing;
#      validate levels/counts/padding. present=count(def==max_def); consume
#      exactly that many values/indices, not num_rows or num_values unconditionally.
#   6. Validate dictionary order/index bounds, variable lengths, delta count and
#      miniblock padding, and complete physical-value payload consumption.
#      Only final hybrid bit-packed padding may exceed the requested count.
#   7. Preserve cross-page V1 rows; enforce V2/OffsetIndex row starts. Join leaf
#      cursors by row/repetition events, never by page ordinal or slot index.
#      Keep null container, empty container, and null element distinct.
#   8. Treat statistics/index/bloom pruning as conservative: unknown order/count
#      means retain data. Honor logical signedness/decimal order and NaN rules.
#      A green local round trip is not enough; test external readers/writers
#      and byte-pinned examples, including malformed files and all-null pages.
#   9. Writer: count slot/present/row totals separately, emit exact physical
#      encodings/codec metadata, finalize dictionary before data pages, compute
#      logical-order stats, and publish footer only after all offsets are final.
#
#
# ==============================================================================
# Helper macros
# ==============================================================================
# Unsigned integers in little-endian byte order
u8(values)  = uint(8, values);
u16(values) = ordered(uint(16, values));
u32(values) = ordered(uint(32, values));
u64(values) = ordered(uint(64, values));

# Signed integers in little-endian byte order
i32(values) = ordered(sint(32, values));
i64(values) = ordered(sint(64, values));

# Floating point in little-endian byte order
f32(values) = ordered(float(32, values));
f64(values) = ordered(float(64, values));
